Image reader bounds (imagebounds)
=================================

Checks the image readers (GIF, BMP, PBM/PGM/PPM, XPM, XBM, PNG, JPEG,
MNG) against crafted files whose header fields ask for huge bitmaps,
frames outside the bitmap, color indexes beyond the color table and the
like, and records what the readers make of ordinary images, so that a
change to a reader can be compared with the reader before it. Not part
of the normal build.

    tests/imagebounds/build.sh [outdir] [compiler flags]
    build/imagebounds crafted [CASE]    the crafted cases (table below)
    build/imagebounds one CASE load|decode
                                        one case in this process (debugger)
    build/imagebounds write DIR         the crafted files, named as the cases
    build/imagebounds gen DIR           synthetic ordinary GIF/BMP/PNM/XPM/XBM
    build/imagebounds dump FILE...      digests (see below)
    tests/imagebounds/mkcorpus.sh DIR [large]
                                        ordinary PNG/JPEG/BMP/GIF/MNG/... made
                                        with ImageMagick and cjpeg ("large":
                                        12000x8000 JPEG, 16384x16384 gray and
                                        16000x16000 RGB PNG)

No X server is needed (QApplication without GUI). The JPEG and MNG
readers are plugins: IMGB_PLUGINS=<dir> makes the program load them from
<dir>/imageformats (the plugins of the library under test).
IMGB_TINY_JPEG, IMGB_TINY_PJPEG and IMGB_TINY_MJPEG name a small
baseline, a small progressive and a small sequential multi-scan JPEG for
the JPEG cases, which patch their frame size; without them the JPEG
cases are left out. E.g.:

    cjpeg tiny.ppm > tiny.jpg
    cjpeg -progressive tiny.ppm > tiny-prog.jpg
    printf '0;\n1;\n2;\n' > scans.txt
    cjpeg -scans scans.txt tiny.ppm > tiny-multiscan.jpg  (one scan per component)

"crafted" runs every case in its own process, once through QImage::load()
(a file) and, for GIF, PNG and MNG, once through a QImageDecoder fed in
2048 byte pieces over all frames, as QMovie and KHTML read images. It
prints the result (null or the size), the largest single allocation
asked for and the peak of live heap (the program counts its own malloc
family; "-" in an ASan build), the process's peak RSS and a verdict: FAIL
for a signal, a sanitizer report (exit 1), a result other than the
expected one or an allocation beyond 64 MB. Some cases also check the
number of frames the decoder delivered and one pixel of the last image
(of the decoder's image when the format has a decoder, else of load()'s);
a mismatch shows in the result column (",frames=N", ",pixel=AARRGGBB").
A case with parameters (the multi-scan JPEG) is read through QImageIO
with them instead of QImage::load(). The image is then read pixel
by pixel and converted to 32 bpp, as any user of it would. Before a fix
some cases allocate gigabytes (the program limits its address space to
6 GiB): run them in a sandbox with memory to spare.

"dump" prints, for each file, QImage::load()'s result (size, depth,
colors, alpha, a hash of the pixels and the color table) and, for GIF,
PNG and MNG, every call of the decoder's consumer (sizes, changed
rectangles, frame periods, the hash at each frame). Run it with the old
and the new library (LD_LIBRARY_PATH; the program finds the library
through RUNPATH) and diff the outputs. MNG frame periods depend on the
clock and differ between runs.

Before/after of the commits that added this test (gif-bounds branch over
e95a7cf), crafted (plain build / ASan build of the library):

    case                          before                          after
    gif-offset-bomb (29 bytes)    20001x20001, 1526 MB            null
    gif-later-frame (decode)      SIGSEGV / heap write            2010x2010
    gif-later-frame-restorebg     SIGSEGV / heap write            2010x2010
    gif-screen-1g                 16384x16384, 1024 MB            null
    gif-8193x8192                 256 MB                          null
    gif-empty-dispose (decode)    SIGSEGV (null jump table)       null
    gif-interlace-outside         ASan negative-size memcpy       100x100
    gif-lzw-cycle (decode)        stack[8192] written (no report: 200x200
                                  inside the object), pixels differ
    bmp-clrused-huge              asks for 8192 MB                null
    bmp-rle8-1g                   1103 MB                         null
    bmp-rle8-eol                  ASan heap read before line 0    1x1
    bmp-index-beyond              ASan read beyond color table    8x1
    bmp-1bpp-one-color            ASan read beyond color table    8x1
    ppm-1g                        1103 MB                         null
    pgm-sample-beyond             ASan read beyond color table    16x1
